  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1050 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nABUNDANT MOISTURE THROUGH THURSDAY:\n\nWe remain under a deep plume of sub-tropical moisture with a\nrobust low pressure trough to our northwest and high pressure to\nthe southeast. PWAT anomalies are near 200 percent of normal and\nare expected to increase overnight through Thursday morning\nacross southwest Colorado upwards of 250 to almost 300 percent\nof normal. At this magnitude, PWAT values are likely to break\nrecords. Much of southeast Utah and especially southwest\nColorado has seen plenty of rainfall and remains saturated.\nCurrently, this area is becoming more stratiform with robust\nthunderstorm development focused on the leading edge of that\nmoisture mainly along and north of I-70 where skies weren't as\nclouded over. This area remains in the right entrance region of\nthe upper level jet so plenty of dynamics to support updrafts\nand thunderstorm activity through the overnight, although most\nhi-res CAMs are showing this area eventually stratiforming out\nby Thursday morning. We are still seeing moderate rainfall rates\nout of the stratiform rain showers with heavier rates where\nstronger convection is occurring and this will be the case\nthrough Thursday. Driving much of this thunderstorm activity is\na robust shortwave moving through tonight as well as remnant\noutflow boundaries from previous convection helping to sustain\nthunderstorm activity through the overnight hours. \n\nExpect this shower activity to continue through the day Thursday\nwith perhaps more stratiform mode to shower activity with\nembedded thunderstorms where breaks occur and can realize the\ninstability. Due to overnight shower and storm activity,\nanticipate that abundant cloud cover Thursday morning may lead\nto this type of scenario. Regardless, robust convection is still\npossible with flash flooding concerns due to already saturated\nsoils, especially across southwest Colorado. Therefore the Flood\nWatch remains in effect through Thursday night. The hail and\nwind threat though should be less as the atmosphere is saturated\nwith hydrologic threats taking precedence. \n\nFRIDAY INTO THE WEEKEND AND BEYOND:\n\nBy Friday, the moisture pool driving this wet pattern rapidly \ndiminishes. However, even with this decrease in moisture, we \nremain confident that the majority of the region will still see \nabove normal PWATs through the weekend with scattered storm \ncoverage. Friday will be in the transition period as the low \npressure begins to track eastward and lifts across the area. \nGiven the drier air advecting from the west with moisture still \nin place, we could see stronger storms on the periphery with \nmore sun to act on the instability and moisture in place. \nHowever, by the weekend, storm activity should become less \npronounced with scattered coverage. By the end of the weekend, \nstorm activity looks to be mostly confined to the higher terrain\nas convection will mainly be driven by orographics. \n\nBeyond the weekend, models indicate some shortwave ridging\nproviding a bit of a break in the active stormy pattern Sunday\nthrough Monday but this looks short lived as models are\nindicating another low pressure trough moving into the Great\nBasin and increasing storm coverage once again early to mid next\nweek.",
